================================================================================
LVM (LOGICAL VOLUME MANAGER) DIRECTORY STRUCTURE & CONFIGURATION
================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW OF /etc/lvm/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The `/etc/lvm/` directory houses the global configurations, historical metadata 
archives, runtime cache files, and performance profiles for the Linux Logical 
Volume Manager (LVM). 

LVM allows administrators to combine physical hard drives into volume groups 
(VG) and dynamically allocate space to logical volumes (LV). The files in this 
directory ensure that volume group structures are maintained, cached, backed up, 
and optimized.


2. DIRECTORY BREAKDOWN & FILE EXPLANATIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A. archive/
   - Description: Stores historical snapshots of volume group metadata.
   - Purpose    : Every time an LVM modification occurs (e.g., resizing an LV, 
                  extending a VG, or adding a PV), LVM saves a timestamped 
                  backup of the previous configuration here.
   - Example    : `almalinux_00000-857093324.vg`
   - Use Case   : Rollback and disaster recovery when an accidental configuration 
                  change or corruption occurs.

B. backup/
   - Description: Stores the latest active structural metadata for each Volume Group.
   - Purpose    : Contains plain-text blueprints of current Volume Group structures. 
                  Unlike `archive/` (which keeps full history), `backup/` maintains 
                  only the current state.
   - Example    : `almalinux`
   - Use Case   : Used by administrative tools like `vgcfgrestore` to restore 
                  corrupted volume group headers directly onto physical disks.

C. cache/
   - Description: Holds persistent LVM runtime cache data.
   - Purpose    : Speeds up system boot and LVM command execution by storing 
                  scanning hints and known block device mappings, preventing LVM 
                  from having to rescan all physical disks repeatedly.

D. devices/
   - Description: Contains hardware device tracking files.
   - File       : `system.devices`
   - Purpose    : Modern LVM uses device tracking files to explicitly bind LVM 
                  Physical Volumes to unique device IDs rather than relying solely 
                  on disk scanning heuristics.

E. lvm.conf & lvmlocal.conf
   - lvm.conf      : The primary global configuration file. Defines scanning filters, 
                     logging levels, allocation policies, backup retention count, 
                     and system-wide LVM daemons.
   - lvmlocal.conf : Contains host-specific configuration overrides that apply ONLY 
                     to the local machine. Used when settings should not be shared 
                     across clustered environments or host migrations.

F. profile/
   - Description: Preset configuration templates for specialized volume behavior 
                  and performance tuning.
   - Key Profiles:
     * cache-mq.profile / cache-smq.profile : Settings for fast SSD caching over 
                                              slower HDDs using Multi-Queue algorithms.
     * thin-generic.profile / thin-performance.profile : Allocation and discard 
                                                          rules for thin pools.
     * vdo-small.profile : Optimized parameters for Virtual Data Optimizer (VDO) 
                           deduplication and compression.
     * command_profile_template.profile / metadata_profile_template.profile : 
       Templates for customizing command execution and metadata validation rules.


3. KEY RECOVERY & MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- View Volume Group Backups:
    cat /etc/lvm/backup/<volume_group_name>

- Restore Metadata from Backup File:
    vgcfgrestore -f /etc/lvm/backup/<volume_group_name> <volume_group_name>

- Restore Metadata from Archive File:
    vgcfgrestore -f /etc/lvm/archive/<archive_file_name>.vg <volume_group_name>

- Check Active LVM Devices:
    lvmdevices --list
